# Pure regenerable pipeline outputs -- re-run run_pipeline.py / export_akn.py
# / export_markdown.py to recreate, never needed just to run review.py, so
# there's nothing to gain from committing them.
data/extracted/
data/diagnostics/
data/akn/
data/markdown/

# export_static_site.py's own output -- rebuilt by .github/workflows/pages.yml
# on every push, never committed.
_site/

# run_ai_review.py's own stdout/stderr, captured per-Act by dashboard.py
# so a failed AI scan's reason (e.g. Ollama not installed) is visible in
# the UI -- not durable state, just the latest run's output.
data/ai_scan_logs/
# The published-site rebuild's own stdout (dashboard.py's /api/site/rebuild).
# Under data/ because that is where this project's working files live, and
# ignored because data/ is what the dashboard's Push button commits -- a
# build log riding along in every push would be noise in the history.
data/site_build.log
# The search index (corpus/search.py). Derived from a merge of two things
# that ARE committed -- the parses and the review database -- so a copy in
# here would be a third that can disagree with them, and sync.pull
# replaces the database wholesale, so the copy that arrived would be
# whichever machine built it last, looking authoritative. It rebuilds from
# scratch in a few seconds.
data/search.db
data/search.building
# The embedding model (download_search_model.py) and the vectors built
# from it (corpus/embeddings.py). Both derived, both large -- 110 MB and
# 19 MB -- and the model is somebody else's artefact. A clone that only
# wants to parse a PDF should not carry either.
models/
data/search_vectors.npy
data/search_vectors.npy.building
data/search_vectors.json

# data/parsed/<act>.json (the raw parse) IS committed, deliberately:
# review.py's verified rows are keyed by a *positional* index into that
# exact parse, so a clone that regenerated it from a later parser version
# could silently misalign verified content with the wrong provisions.
#
# data/legislation.db is not, any more. It is the working store several
# processes write to while you review -- and it is rebuilt from
# data/review/**.jsonl, which is the same review work written as text git
# can merge, one line per provision (see corpus/review_sync.py). Keeping
# the binary here as well would be a second copy of the same state that
# can disagree with the first, and it was the binary that forced every
# pull to be fast-forward only: git cannot merge two versions of it, so
# reviewing on the server while a code change landed elsewhere was enough
# to jam the sync.
#
# A fresh clone therefore has the review work but no database. Build one:
#
#     python3 -m corpus.review_sync import
#
# What the export last left on disk (corpus/review_sync.py), so that a
# `git pull` bringing newer review work than this database holds is
# noticed instead of being written over. Machine state, like the
# database it guards.
data/review-sync-state.json

data/legislation.db
data/legislation.db-wal
data/legislation.db-shm

# Superseded by data/legislation.db above; only relevant if you still
# have pre-migration JSON files around (see migrate_json_to_sqlite.py).
data/verified/
data/links/
data/corrections.jsonl

__pycache__/
*.pyc
.idea/
.pytest_cache/

# dashboard.py's persisted login credentials (username + salted password
# hash, written after the forced first-login password change) -- never
# commit this, even though it's a hash rather than the plain password.
.dashboard_auth.json

# deploy/dashboard.env.example is tracked as a template; the real copy
# (deploy/README.md step 4) holds actual credentials and must not be.
deploy/dashboard.env
# Same again for the published site's own passphrase.
deploy/site.env

# Databases kept aside by a discard from the dashboard (corpus/sync.py).
# Recoverable work, deliberately not committed: a backup that travelled
# with the repository would be another copy of the thing it backs up.
_backups/

# On the server, the `dashboard` service account's home directory IS this
# checkout (/opt/corpusvic -- see deploy/README.md), so the things a home
# directory holds sit inside the working tree:
#
#   .ssh/    the GitHub deploy key. A PRIVATE key, in a directory git
#            would otherwise consider its business. Without this line a
#            `git add -A` on the server stages it, and the only thing
#            standing between that and a published private key is
#            GitHub's push protection -- which is a backstop, not a
#            design. It has caught it once already.
#   .venv/   the Python environment. Not secret, just thousands of files
#            that belong to the machine rather than the repository.
#
# The pre-commit hook (deploy/githooks/pre-commit) refuses a staged
# private key as well, because an ignore rule only helps while it is
# there and `git add -f` walks straight past it.
#
# Beware the other direction: because these are untracked, `git clean -fd`
# in this checkout DELETES the deploy key and the environment. Ignoring
# them does not protect them -- `-x` is not needed for that, and neither
# is any of this. See deploy/README.md.
.ssh/
.venv/
venv/

# Any private key, wherever it turns up. Belt and braces with .ssh/
# above: the point is that a key can never be staged by accident,
# whatever it is called or wherever somebody put it.
*.pem
*.key
id_rsa*
id_ed25519*
id_ecdsa*
!*.pub

# Cannot re-publish authorised Acts. The PDFs stay on disk and out of
# git; basic-structure.yaml is not one of them -- it is the note on
# Victorian drafting convention that hierarchy.py, rule_parser.py and
# the exporters all cite, so it stays tracked.
acts/*
!acts/profiles/

# The scoreboard's memory of the last run (corpus/teaching/score.py); the
# examples themselves are committed.
data/teaching/.last/
